lesson "Differentiation I — The Derivative & the Power Rule" {
  course: "calculus"
  skills: ["deriv-definition", "deriv-power-rule", "deriv-sum-rule"]

  slide "What Is a Derivative?" {
    id: "d1-intro"
    cat: "Foundations"
    > Calculus is the mathematics of **change**. The **derivative** answers one
    > question: *how fast is a function changing at a single instant?*
    >
    > Geometrically, that instantaneous rate is the **slope of the tangent line** —
    > the straight line that just grazes the curve at a point. Everything in this
    > course is built on that one idea, so let's see it move.
  }

  slide "From Secant to Tangent" {
    id: "d1-secant"
    cat: "Foundations"
    > Pick two points on $f(x) = x^2$ and connect them: that's a **secant** line,
    > and its slope is the *average* rate of change. Now slide the second point in
    > by shrinking $h$. As $h \to 0$, the secant swings into the **tangent** — and
    > its slope closes in on **2**, the true instantaneous rate at $x = 1$.

    scene plane {
      x: [-1, 4]
      y: [-1, 9]
      grid
      axes
      param h = 2 { range: [0.1, 3], step: 0.1 }
      curve f = x^2 { color: primary, width: 3 }
      point A = (1, 1) { color: success, r: 6, label: "x = 1" }
      point B = (1 + h, (1 + h)^2) { color: accent, r: 6 }
      line tangent { through: A, slope: 2, color: success, style: dashed, width: 2 }
      line sec = (1, 1) -> (1 + h, (1 + h)^2) { color: accent, width: 2 }
      label at (1.4, 8) = "secant slope = ${((1 + h)^2 - 1) / h}" { color: accent }
      slider h { label: "gap h" }
    }

    goal "Shrink h below 0.3 and watch the secant become the tangent" {
      when: h < 0.3
      hint: "Drag the slider all the way left."
    }
  }

  slide "The Derivative as a Function" {
    id: "d1-derivfn"
    cat: "Foundations"
    > Do that at *every* point and the slopes trace out a brand-new function: the
    > **derivative** $f'(x)$. For $f(x) = x^2$ the tangent slope at any $x$ is
    > $2x$ — so $f'(x) = 2x$. Drag the point: the height of the dashed line (the
    > slope readout) always equals the value of $f'$ underneath it.
    >
    > We write it $f'(x)$ or $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$.

    scene plane {
      x: [-3, 3]
      y: [-6, 9]
      grid
      axes
      param a = 1 { range: [-3, 3], step: 0.1 }
      curve f = x^2 { color: primary, width: 3 }
      curve fprime = 2*x { color: accent, width: 3, style: dashed }
      point P = (a, a^2) { color: primary, r: 7, drag: x -> a, label: "(a, a²)" }
      point S = (a, 2*a) { color: accent, r: 6, label: "f'(a) = ${2*a}" }
      line tangent { through: P, slope: 2*a, color: success, width: 2 }
      slider a { label: "a" }
    }
  }

  slide "The Power Rule" {
    id: "d1-power"
    cat: "Power Rule"
    > Here is the workhorse of differentiation. For any power of $x$:
    >
    > $$\frac{d}{dx}\left[x^{n}\right] = n\,x^{\,n-1}$$
    >
    > In words: **bring the exponent down in front, then knock the exponent down
    > by one.** Move the slider to change $n$ and watch the blue curve $x^n$ and its
    > derivative (dashed) change together.

    scene plane {
      x: [-2, 2]
      y: [-8, 12]
      grid
      axes
      param n = 2 { range: [1, 4], step: 1 }
      curve f = x^n { color: primary, width: 3 }
      curve fprime = n * x^(n - 1) { color: accent, width: 3, style: dashed }
      label at (-1.9, 11) = "f = xⁿ    f' = n·xⁿ⁻¹" { color: neutral }
      slider n { label: "power n" }
    }
  }

  slide "Quick Check: Power Rule" {
    id: "d1-quiz-power"
    cat: "Power Rule"
    quiz {
      ask "Using the power rule, what is $\frac{d}{dx}\left[x^{5}\right]$?"
      - "$x^4$" { why: "Don't forget to bring the 5 down in front." }
      * "$5x^4$"
      - "$5x^5$" { why: "The exponent drops by one: 5 − 1 = 4." }
      - "$4x^5$"
      hint "Bring the power down, then subtract one from it."
      ! "Bring down the 5, drop the power to 4: $5x^4$."
    }
  }

  slide "Evaluate a Derivative" {
    id: "d1-num-cube"
    cat: "Power Rule"
    numeric {
      ask "If $f(x) = x^{3}$, compute $f'(2)$."
      answer: 12
      hint "First differentiate: $f'(x) = 3x^2$."
      ! "$f'(x) = 3x^2$, so $f'(2) = 3\cdot 4 = 12$."
    }
  }

  slide "Sum, Difference & Constant Multiple" {
    id: "d1-sumrule"
    cat: "Combining Terms"
    > Derivatives split neatly across $+$ and $-$, and constants come along for the
    > ride:
    >
    > $$\left[f \pm g\right]' = f' \pm g' \qquad \left[c\,f\right]' = c\,f'$$
    >
    > So you can differentiate a polynomial **one term at a time**. For
    > $g(x) = 3x^2 + 2x - 5$: the $3x^2$ gives $6x$, the $2x$ gives $2$, and the
    > constant $-5$ gives $0$ — so $g'(x) = 6x + 2$.
  }

  slide "Differentiate a Polynomial" {
    id: "d1-num-poly"
    cat: "Combining Terms"
    numeric {
      ask "Let $g(x) = 3x^{2} + 2x - 5$. Find $g'(1)$."
      answer: 8
      hint "Differentiate term by term: $g'(x) = 6x + 2$."
      ! "$g'(x) = 6x + 2$, so $g'(1) = 6 + 2 = 8$."
    }
  }

  slide "Build the Derivative" {
    id: "d1-build"
    cat: "Combining Terms"
    build {
      ask "Assemble $\frac{d}{dx}\left[x^{4}\right]$ from the tiles."
      bank: ["4", "x", "^", "3", "2"]
      answer: ["4", "x", "^", "3"]
      ! "Bring the 4 down, drop the power by one: $4x^3$."
    }
  }
}
